1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,140
There are tens of thousands of cryptocurrencies.

2
00:00:03,160 --> 00:00:04,870
By some counts, millions.

3
00:00:06,130 --> 00:00:07,490
But almost all of them fit

4
00:00:07,510 --> 00:00:08,830
into one of twelve boxes.

5
00:00:10,070 --> 00:00:10,920
Learn the boxes,

6
00:00:10,940 --> 00:00:12,510
and you'll understand almost

7
00:00:12,530 --> 00:00:13,620
any coin you hear about.

8
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:14,880
In 12 minutes.

9
00:00:16,130 --> 00:00:18,380
No hype, and no price predictions.

10
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:21,050
Crypto can lose most of its value, fast.

11
00:00:21,070 --> 00:00:23,680
This is education, not financial advice.

12
00:00:24,930 --> 00:00:26,650
Let's open the first box.

13
00:00:27,900 --> 00:00:28,560
Box 1.

14
00:00:28,580 --> 00:00:29,310
Bitcoin.

15
00:00:30,570 --> 00:00:32,950
Bitcoin is the first cryptocurrency.

16
00:00:32,970 --> 00:00:35,000
It launched in 2009,

17
00:00:35,020 --> 00:00:37,690
created by someone called Satoshi Nakamoto.

18
00:00:38,930 --> 00:00:41,240
Nobody knows who Satoshi really is.

19
00:00:41,260 --> 00:00:44,310
They disappeared in 2011, and never came back.

20
00:00:45,570 --> 00:00:47,380
The idea is a public ledger,

21
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:49,530
copied on thousands of computers,

22
00:00:49,550 --> 00:00:52,150
where nobody can secretly rewrite the past.

23
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:55,430
New blocks are added by miners.

24
00:00:55,450 --> 00:00:57,650
They burn electricity to solve a puzzle,

25
00:00:57,670 --> 00:00:59,330
and the winner earns new bitcoin.

26
00:01:00,570 --> 00:01:01,930
And here's the key rule.

27
00:01:01,950 --> 00:01:02,600
There will never

28
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:04,670
be more than 21 million bitcoin.

29
00:01:05,930 --> 00:01:07,260
About every four years,

30
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:09,570
the reward for miners is cut in half.

31
00:01:09,590 --> 00:01:11,060
This is called the halving.

32
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:14,530
The last one, in April 2024,

33
00:01:14,550 --> 00:01:18,050
cut it to 3.125 bitcoin per block.

34
00:01:18,070 --> 00:01:19,200
The very last bitcoin

35
00:01:19,220 --> 00:01:21,660
should be mined around 2140.

36
00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:25,230
That's why people call it digital gold.

37
00:01:25,250 --> 00:01:26,490
Scarce, hard to change,

38
00:01:26,510 --> 00:01:28,460
and it doesn't pay you anything.

39
00:01:29,700 --> 00:01:30,930
But it's volatile.

40
00:01:30,950 --> 00:01:33,310
Bitcoin has lost more than 70%

41
00:01:33,330 --> 00:01:34,840
of its value several times,

42
00:01:34,860 --> 00:01:36,830
and recovered each time, so far.

43
00:01:38,070 --> 00:01:38,760
Box 2.

44
00:01:38,780 --> 00:01:39,630
Ethereum.

45
00:01:40,870 --> 00:01:42,940
Ethereum launched in 2015.

46
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:44,870
Its big idea was simple.

47
00:01:44,890 --> 00:01:46,920
What if a blockchain could run programs,

48
00:01:46,940 --> 00:01:48,080
not just payments?

49
00:01:49,330 --> 00:01:51,880
These programs are called smart contracts.

50
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:54,640
Once they're live, they run exactly as written,

51
00:01:54,660 --> 00:01:56,160
with no company in the middle.

52
00:01:57,400 --> 00:02:00,670
Every action on Ethereum costs a small fee,

53
00:02:00,690 --> 00:02:02,400
called gas, paid in its own coin, ether.

54
00:02:03,630 --> 00:02:05,420
A lot of what's in the next boxes,

55
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:09,060
stablecoins, DeFi, NFTs, grew up on Ethereum.

56
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:14,250
In September 2022, Ethereum changed how it works.

57
00:02:14,270 --> 00:02:14,960
It stopped mining,

58
00:02:14,980 --> 00:02:16,870
and switched to proof of stake.

59
00:02:18,130 --> 00:02:19,900
Instead of burning electricity,

60
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:22,590
people lock up their ether as a guarantee.

61
00:02:22,610 --> 00:02:26,410
Energy use fell by about 99.95%.

62
00:02:27,670 --> 00:02:29,610
And one big difference with Bitcoin.

63
00:02:29,630 --> 00:02:32,090
Ether has no fixed maximum supply.

64
00:02:33,330 --> 00:02:33,930
Box 3.

65
00:02:33,950 --> 00:02:35,340
The other blockchains.

66
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:39,670
Solana, Cardano, Avalanche, Tron, and many more.

67
00:02:39,690 --> 00:02:41,170
Each one is its own blockchain,

68
00:02:41,190 --> 00:02:42,340
with its own coin.

69
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:45,600
Most of them make the same promise.

70
00:02:45,620 --> 00:02:47,710
Faster and cheaper than Ethereum.

71
00:02:48,970 --> 00:02:51,590
The trade-off is usually decentralisation.

72
00:02:51,610 --> 00:02:54,490
Fewer, more powerful computers run the network.

73
00:02:55,730 --> 00:02:57,860
Solana is the best known example.

74
00:02:57,880 --> 00:02:59,160
It's fast and cheap,

75
00:02:59,180 --> 00:03:01,650
but its network has gone down several times.

76
00:03:02,900 --> 00:03:05,490
In 2017, dozens of coins were

77
00:03:05,510 --> 00:03:07,000
sold as the next Ethereum.

78
00:03:07,020 --> 00:03:08,810
Many are now barely used.

79
00:03:10,070 --> 00:03:11,750
So when a new chain promises

80
00:03:11,770 --> 00:03:12,990
to change everything,

81
00:03:13,010 --> 00:03:13,840
ask one question.

82
00:03:13,860 --> 00:03:15,300
Who actually uses it?

83
00:03:16,530 --> 00:03:17,180
Box 4.

84
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:18,060
Layer 2s.

85
00:03:19,300 --> 00:03:20,600
Ethereum can get slow

86
00:03:20,620 --> 00:03:22,360
and expensive when it's busy.

87
00:03:22,380 --> 00:03:24,700
So people built networks on top of it.

88
00:03:25,930 --> 00:03:28,850
These layer 2s collect thousands of transactions,

89
00:03:28,870 --> 00:03:30,170
bundle them together,

90
00:03:30,190 --> 00:03:32,380
and post the result back on Ethereum.

91
00:03:33,630 --> 00:03:36,830
The big ones are called Arbitrum, Optimism,

92
00:03:36,850 --> 00:03:38,510
and Base, which was built by Coinbase.

93
00:03:39,770 --> 00:03:40,640
On a layer 2,

94
00:03:40,660 --> 00:03:42,300
a fee can drop from a few dollars

95
00:03:42,320 --> 00:03:43,150
to a few cents.

96
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:46,410
Bitcoin has its own version too,

97
00:03:46,430 --> 00:03:47,460
the Lightning Network,

98
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:49,420
made for small and fast payments.

99
00:03:50,670 --> 00:03:51,370
The point is,

100
00:03:51,390 --> 00:03:52,970
layer 2s aren't a new coin

101
00:03:52,990 --> 00:03:53,620
to bet on.

102
00:03:53,640 --> 00:03:54,530
They're plumbing.

103
00:03:55,770 --> 00:03:56,500
Box 5.

104
00:03:56,520 --> 00:03:57,570
Stablecoins.

105
00:03:58,830 --> 00:04:00,970
A stablecoin is a token that's meant

106
00:04:00,990 --> 00:04:02,460
to always be worth one dollar,

107
00:04:02,480 --> 00:04:03,480
or one euro.

108
00:04:04,730 --> 00:04:06,300
How? The company behind it

109
00:04:06,320 --> 00:04:08,120
holds real money in reserve,

110
00:04:08,140 --> 00:04:10,770
mostly cash and short-term government bonds.

111
00:04:12,030 --> 00:04:14,120
The two giants are USDT,

112
00:04:14,140 --> 00:04:15,660
from a company called Tether,

113
00:04:15,680 --> 00:04:17,470
and USDC, from Circle.

114
00:04:18,730 --> 00:04:21,400
Traders use them to park money between trades,

115
00:04:21,420 --> 00:04:23,070
without going back to the bank.

116
00:04:24,300 --> 00:04:26,950
But not every stablecoin holds real money.

117
00:04:26,970 --> 00:04:29,120
In 2022, one called

118
00:04:29,140 --> 00:04:31,670
TerraUSD used an algorithm instead.

119
00:04:32,930 --> 00:04:35,670
In May 2022, it lost its peg.

120
00:04:35,690 --> 00:04:35,920
In a few days,

121
00:04:35,940 --> 00:04:39,300
about 40 billion dollars of value vanished.

122
00:04:40,570 --> 00:04:43,780
Its sister coin, LUNA, fell more than 99%.

123
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:45,780
Many people lost their savings.

124
00:04:47,030 --> 00:04:47,710
Europe reacted.

125
00:04:47,730 --> 00:04:50,200
Under MiCA, the EU's crypto law,

126
00:04:50,220 --> 00:04:55,190
stablecoin issuers have needed authorisation since June 2024.

127
00:04:56,430 --> 00:04:59,630
Tether's USDT doesn't have that authorisation.

128
00:04:59,650 --> 00:05:01,230
So the big exchanges removed

129
00:05:01,250 --> 00:05:03,620
or restricted it for users in Europe.

130
00:05:04,870 --> 00:05:07,160
Circle's USDC is authorised.

131
00:05:07,180 --> 00:05:10,440
And there are euro stablecoins too, like EURC.

132
00:05:11,700 --> 00:05:12,360
Box 6.

133
00:05:12,380 --> 00:05:13,780
Exchange tokens.

134
00:05:15,030 --> 00:05:17,820
Some crypto exchanges create their own coin.

135
00:05:17,840 --> 00:05:20,570
The best known is BNB, from Binance.

136
00:05:21,830 --> 00:05:23,610
Holding it can give you perks,

137
00:05:23,630 --> 00:05:25,000
like lower trading fees.

138
00:05:25,020 --> 00:05:26,350
But its value depends

139
00:05:26,370 --> 00:05:28,160
a lot on the exchange itself.

140
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:30,750
That link can be deadly.

141
00:05:30,770 --> 00:05:32,330
Remember FTX?

142
00:05:33,570 --> 00:05:36,000
In November 2022, FTX,

143
00:05:36,020 --> 00:05:38,760
one of the biggest exchanges in the world,

144
00:05:38,780 --> 00:05:40,270
collapsed in a matter of days.

145
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:42,770
Its own token, FTT,

146
00:05:42,790 --> 00:05:44,120
crashed by more than

147
00:05:44,140 --> 00:05:46,060
90% in a few days.

148
00:05:47,300 --> 00:05:48,750
Then the truth came out.

149
00:05:48,770 --> 00:05:50,130
Around 8 billion dollars

150
00:05:50,150 --> 00:05:51,810
of customer money was missing.

151
00:05:53,070 --> 00:05:55,180
Its founder, Sam Bankman-Fried,

152
00:05:55,200 --> 00:05:58,000
was later sentenced to 25 years in prison.

153
00:05:59,270 --> 00:06:00,210
So remember.

154
00:06:00,230 --> 00:06:01,690
A token tied to one company

155
00:06:01,710 --> 00:06:03,710
is only as safe as that company.

156
00:06:04,970 --> 00:06:05,730
Box 7.

157
00:06:05,750 --> 00:06:08,280
DeFi, short for decentralised finance.

158
00:06:09,530 --> 00:06:11,140
It's banking without a bank.

159
00:06:11,160 --> 00:06:13,020
Lending, borrowing and trading,

160
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:14,720
all run by smart contracts.

161
00:06:15,970 --> 00:06:18,310
On Uniswap, you swap one token for another,

162
00:06:18,330 --> 00:06:20,510
with no company holding your money.

163
00:06:20,530 --> 00:06:23,390
On Aave, you can lend and earn interest.

164
00:06:24,630 --> 00:06:26,310
Their tokens often give holders

165
00:06:26,330 --> 00:06:27,910
a vote on how the app is run.

166
00:06:29,170 --> 00:06:29,790
The catch?

167
00:06:29,810 --> 00:06:31,160
If there's a bug in the code,

168
00:06:31,180 --> 00:06:32,410
there's no bank to call,

169
00:06:32,430 --> 00:06:33,760
and nobody to refund you.

170
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:36,290
And hackers know it.

171
00:06:36,310 --> 00:06:38,060
In 2022 alone,

172
00:06:38,080 --> 00:06:41,010
billions of dollars were stolen from crypto projects,

173
00:06:41,030 --> 00:06:42,450
much of it from DeFi.

174
00:06:43,700 --> 00:06:44,810
DeFi is a real

175
00:06:44,830 --> 00:06:47,290
experiment in finance without middlemen.

176
00:06:47,310 --> 00:06:49,410
Just know there's no safety net.

177
00:06:50,670 --> 00:06:51,400
Box 8.

178
00:06:51,420 --> 00:06:52,310
Memecoins.

179
00:06:53,570 --> 00:06:56,090
It started in 2013 with Dogecoin,

180
00:06:56,110 --> 00:06:57,780
a coin made as a joke,

181
00:06:57,800 --> 00:07:00,090
with a Shiba Inu dog as its logo.

182
00:07:01,330 --> 00:07:04,280
Then came Shiba Inu, Pepe, and thousands more,

183
00:07:04,300 --> 00:07:07,110
named after dogs, frogs, and internet jokes.

184
00:07:08,370 --> 00:07:11,230
A memecoin has no product, and no profits.

185
00:07:11,250 --> 00:07:14,040
Its price depends on one thing only, attention.

186
00:07:15,300 --> 00:07:16,020
Today, anyone

187
00:07:16,040 --> 00:07:17,900
can create one in about a minute,

188
00:07:17,920 --> 00:07:19,010
for almost nothing.

189
00:07:19,030 --> 00:07:20,640
Millions have been launched.

190
00:07:21,900 --> 00:07:23,770
Most of them fade to almost nothing,

191
00:07:23,790 --> 00:07:25,230
often within days.

192
00:07:25,250 --> 00:07:26,570
Some were built from day one

193
00:07:26,590 --> 00:07:27,710
to take your money.

194
00:07:28,970 --> 00:07:31,090
A few early buyers make a fortune.

195
00:07:31,110 --> 00:07:32,080
Most people who buy

196
00:07:32,100 --> 00:07:33,660
after the hype lose money.

197
00:07:34,900 --> 00:07:37,210
So buying a memecoin isn't investing.

198
00:07:37,230 --> 00:07:37,460
It's a game,

199
00:07:37,480 --> 00:07:39,710
and the odds aren't on your side.

200
00:07:40,970 --> 00:07:41,810
Box 9.

201
00:07:41,830 --> 00:07:42,640
NFTs.

202
00:07:43,900 --> 00:07:46,780
NFT stands for non-fungible token.

203
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:48,360
It's a token that's one of a kind,

204
00:07:48,380 --> 00:07:50,480
like a certificate of ownership.

205
00:07:51,730 --> 00:07:54,620
Usually, the image itself isn't on the blockchain.

206
00:07:54,640 --> 00:07:55,660
Often, the token

207
00:07:55,680 --> 00:07:57,000
is just a link to it.

208
00:07:58,230 --> 00:07:59,870
In March 2021,

209
00:07:59,890 --> 00:08:02,000
an NFT by the artist Beeple sold

210
00:08:02,020 --> 00:08:05,190
at Christie's for about 69 million dollars.

211
00:08:06,430 --> 00:08:07,760
Then the hype faded.

212
00:08:07,780 --> 00:08:09,690
NFT trading volumes fell more than

213
00:08:09,710 --> 00:08:12,860
95% from their 2021 peak.

214
00:08:14,100 --> 00:08:16,660
Some uses may survive, like event tickets,

215
00:08:16,680 --> 00:08:18,360
or items in video games.

216
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:21,850
But most NFTs bought during the hype

217
00:08:21,870 --> 00:08:22,650
are now worth

218
00:08:22,670 --> 00:08:24,470
a fraction of what people paid.

219
00:08:25,730 --> 00:08:26,360
Box 10.

220
00:08:26,380 --> 00:08:27,570
Privacy coins.

221
00:08:28,830 --> 00:08:31,440
On Bitcoin, every transaction is public.

222
00:08:31,460 --> 00:08:32,230
Anyone can follow

223
00:08:32,250 --> 00:08:34,210
the money from wallet to wallet.

224
00:08:35,470 --> 00:08:37,130
Privacy coins hide that.

225
00:08:37,150 --> 00:08:38,890
Monero hides who sent the money,

226
00:08:38,910 --> 00:08:40,740
who received it, and how much.

227
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:43,860
Zcash works in a similar way,

228
00:08:43,880 --> 00:08:45,650
but there, privacy is optional.

229
00:08:46,900 --> 00:08:49,210
Privacy has real, legal uses.

230
00:08:49,230 --> 00:08:51,690
But regulators worry about money laundering.

231
00:08:52,930 --> 00:08:53,740
In the EU,

232
00:08:53,760 --> 00:08:57,600
new anti-money laundering rules apply from July 2027.

233
00:08:58,870 --> 00:08:59,640
From then on,

234
00:08:59,660 --> 00:09:02,100
regulated crypto platforms won't be allowed

235
00:09:02,120 --> 00:09:03,540
to handle privacy coins.

236
00:09:04,800 --> 00:09:07,750
Several big exchanges already dropped them in Europe.

237
00:09:07,770 --> 00:09:09,380
Owning one isn't banned,

238
00:09:09,400 --> 00:09:11,480
but buying or selling is getting harder.

239
00:09:12,730 --> 00:09:13,590
Box 11.

240
00:09:13,610 --> 00:09:14,540
Scam coins.

241
00:09:15,800 --> 00:09:18,210
Some coins exist for one reason only.

242
00:09:18,230 --> 00:09:19,390
To take your money.

243
00:09:20,630 --> 00:09:22,350
The classic is the rug pull.

244
00:09:22,370 --> 00:09:24,440
The creators hype a new token,

245
00:09:24,460 --> 00:09:25,800
wait for people to buy,

246
00:09:25,820 --> 00:09:27,510
then sell everything and vanish.

247
00:09:28,770 --> 00:09:30,880
In 2021, a token named

248
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:33,190
after Squid Game shot up for days.

249
00:09:33,210 --> 00:09:35,580
Then buyers noticed they couldn't sell it.

250
00:09:36,830 --> 00:09:38,360
The creators cashed out,

251
00:09:38,380 --> 00:09:39,230
and the price fell

252
00:09:39,250 --> 00:09:40,750
to almost zero in minutes.

253
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:43,530
And then there's OneCoin.

254
00:09:43,550 --> 00:09:44,410
It was sold across

255
00:09:44,430 --> 00:09:46,160
Europe as the next Bitcoin,

256
00:09:46,180 --> 00:09:48,190
but it never even had a real blockchain.

257
00:09:49,430 --> 00:09:52,260
Investors put in more than 4 billion dollars.

258
00:09:52,280 --> 00:09:54,810
Its founder disappeared in 2017,

259
00:09:54,830 --> 00:09:56,110
and has never been found.

260
00:09:57,370 --> 00:09:59,350
The red flags are always the same.

261
00:09:59,370 --> 00:10:02,140
Guaranteed returns, pressure to buy fast,

262
00:10:02,160 --> 00:10:03,200
a famous face,

263
00:10:03,220 --> 00:10:04,410
and a story that's too good

264
00:10:04,430 --> 00:10:05,150
to be true.

265
00:10:06,400 --> 00:10:08,610
If someone promises to double your money,

266
00:10:08,630 --> 00:10:10,820
the only thing that doubles is the risk.

267
00:10:12,070 --> 00:10:12,770
Box 12.

268
00:10:12,790 --> 00:10:13,960
The digital euro.

269
00:10:15,200 --> 00:10:16,240
It's the last box

270
00:10:16,260 --> 00:10:17,900
because it's the odd one out.

271
00:10:17,920 --> 00:10:20,220
The digital euro isn't crypto at all.

272
00:10:21,470 --> 00:10:22,570
It would be issued

273
00:10:22,590 --> 00:10:24,110
by the European Central Bank.

274
00:10:24,130 --> 00:10:26,890
One digital euro would always be one euro,

275
00:10:26,910 --> 00:10:28,210
because it is the euro.

276
00:10:29,470 --> 00:10:31,250
Think of it as digital cash.

277
00:10:31,270 --> 00:10:32,860
Like the banknotes in your wallet,

278
00:10:32,880 --> 00:10:33,920
but on your phone.

279
00:10:35,170 --> 00:10:36,960
In December 2025,

280
00:10:36,980 --> 00:10:39,670
EU governments agreed on their position.

281
00:10:39,690 --> 00:10:41,740
In 2026, the European

282
00:10:41,760 --> 00:10:43,610
Parliament agreed on its own.

283
00:10:44,870 --> 00:10:47,300
